WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.040
The Incredible Hulk is one of Marvel's oldest
and most recognizable icons.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.030
Created by comics legends Stan Lee and Jack
Kirby and first appearing in 1962's Incredible

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:15.680
Hulk #1, the Green Goliath spawned hundreds
of comics, two live-action movies, a popular

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.720
live-action TV show, and multiple animated
series, films, and video games.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.080
And most importantly — big foam hands that
make smashy sounds when you hit things with

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:25.820
them.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.960
"I've got the power of the Hulk in my hands,
and I like it!"

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:36.120
With so much Hulk over so much media, it's
almost impossible for even the most hardcore

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.679
fans to keep up with all of the stories.

00:00:38.679 --> 00:00:41.730
Here's the untold truth of the Incredible
Hulk.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.440
Banner SMASH!

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.640
One of the strangest and most hilarious periods
of the Hulk's history was the reign of "Savage

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.000
Banner" which turned the most well-known Banner/Hulk
relationship on its head.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.910
Toward the end of 1995's The Incredible Hulk
#425, longtime Hulk writer Peter David changed

00:00:58.910 --> 00:00:59.910
things up.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:04.300
After enjoying a few years in the Hulk's body
while retaining the intelligence of Bruce

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.570
Banner, the Hulk transformed back into Banner,
but gained the simple mentality of the Hulk.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:14.369
For the first time in the history of the character,
anger became the Hulk's greatest weakness.

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:18.800
Previously, even after turning into the Hulk,
the hero's strength would continue to increase

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.909
as he got angry.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:24.440
Now he struggled to stay calm during fights
with the likes of Thor and Man-Thing, so that

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:29.320
he wouldn't get angry and transform into the
utterly helpless Bruce Banner.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.320
Bob Banner

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.409
There are a lot of differences between the
Incredible Hulk comic and the '70s live-action

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:36.520
TV show.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.409
One of the first obvious differences is that
the Hulk's more human half in the show is

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:43.320
named David Banner rather than Bruce Banner.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.800
"David?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:45.810
David?

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.810
David come on."

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:53.189
Kenneth Johnson, who developed the Incredible
Hulk show, explained that he changed the name

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:57.380
because he didn't like the comic book tradition
of using alliteration when naming characters.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.270
"And I was looking to break away from that
tradition so that people wouldn't be thinking

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.049
comic book in any way when they looked at
the show."

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:08.340
Regardless of the reason, Johnson isn't the
first person guilty of changing Bruce Banner's

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.410
first name.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.140
That honor goes to Stan Lee.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:15.810
Lee was writing a lot of comics in the early
and mid '60s and used alliterative names for

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.730
his characters, in part, to help him remember
all the names.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.360
Unfortunately, in the case of the Hulk, this
led him to mistakenly referring to Bruce Banner

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.670
as "Bob" Banner.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:29.630
His solution, announced in a few issues later
in Fantastic Four #28, was that the real full

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:34.940
name of the Hulk's more human alter-ego was
Doctor Robert Bruce Banner.

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:41.050
Hilariously, that wasn't Lee's only name slip-up,
which is why 1963's The Amazing Spider-Man

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.660
featured a few pages of some guy named Peter
Palmer.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.080
Red Hulk's real origin

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.920
In 2008, Hulk was relaunched with the creative
team of Jeph Loeb and Ed McGuinness.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.000
The pair introduced the world to the Red Hulk
– a malevolent, intelligent, and vicious

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.220
red-skinned version of the Hulk who seemed
like he might be even more powerful than the

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:01.740
original.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.630
It took a couple of years, but in Hulk #23
it was revealed the Red Hulk was General Thaddeus

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.800
"Thunderbolt" Ross, the same army general
who'd hounded the original Hulk for years.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.580
Believe it or not, Loeb wasn't the first writer
to think of a red Hulk.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:29.070
Kenneth Johnson, who developed the '70s Incredible
Hulk show, thought a red Hulk made more sense

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.260
since it's the color most people associate
with anger.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.160
"YAH!"

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:40.180
But Stan Lee was adamant that the green Hulk
was too iconic an image to be changed, so

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:46.090
they painted Lou Ferrigno green and Red Hulk
went back into hiding for another 30 years.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.680
Many voices

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.350
While Lou Ferrigno voiced the Hulk for the
'90s Incredible Hulk cartoon and in most Marvel

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.840
Studios films featuring the Hulk, he wasn't
the Hulk's voice in The Incredible Hulk TV

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:57.840
show.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.950
The one where he, you know, actually played
the Hulk.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:04.980
The source of the '70s Incredible Hulk's grunts
and growls was Ted Cassidy, best known as

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.010
Lurch from The Addams Family.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.910
Cassidy also read the narration that told
the story of David Banner in the beginning

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:11.910
of each episode.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:17.600
"Dr. David Banner, physician, scientist, searching
for a way to tap into the hidden strengths

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.540
that all humans have."

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.820
After Cassidy passed away in 1979, Charles
Napier replaced him as the voice of The Incredible

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.180
Hulk.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.440
Psychic senses

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:31.259
You might think the Hulk's abilities are limited
to the physical, but there's more to the big

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:33.280
green guy than just pounding on stuff.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.889
Throughout his comics, the Hulk has shown
some strange, almost psychic abilities.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:42.419
During one of the Hulk's first encounters
with Doctor Strange in Marvel Feature #1,

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:47.080
Strange learned that the Hulk could somehow
see and interact with Strange's astral form.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:51.650
Peter David later made use of this ability,
hounding the Hulk with ghosts both literal

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.560
and metaphorical during his run on Incredible
Hulk.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.919
On top of that, the Hulk has always instinctively
known how to return to the gamma bomb site

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:00.110
where he was created.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:04.650
More than 100 issues later, this was explained
as being a kind of psychic energy summons

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.969
from the Maestro — a future version of the
Hulk who was defeated by being teleported

00:05:08.969 --> 00:05:11.540
back in time to the gamma bomb blast.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.780
But this doesn't explain the Hulk's other
geographic instincts, such as in Incredible

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:21.031
Hulk #33 when the Hulk is able to leap from
the coast of Wakanda and instinctively know

00:05:21.031 --> 00:05:26.720
how to angle his jump in order to land on
a tiny atoll in the middle of the ocean.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.240
War horse

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.900
Beyond his ongoing rivalry with Wolverine,
the Hulk doesn't have a lot of connections

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:33.900
with the X-Men.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.150
So it would likely surprise even Wolverine
to know that the Hulk was briefly the herald

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:42.069
of one of their most powerful adversaries:
Apocalypse.

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:47.389
In Incredible Hulk #456, Apocalypse captured
the Hulk and transformed him into one of his

00:05:47.389 --> 00:05:53.430
Four Horsemen — making the Hulk the first
non-mutant character to be a Horseman of Apocalypse.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.080
Apocalypse's transformation included cybernetic
implants in the Hulk's brain, a big sword,

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.199
and a retractable metal whip.

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:04.449
The Hulk's added power allowed him to easily
dispatch with the twin threats of Juggernaut

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:09.379
and the Absorbing Man — either of which
would be a match for the Hulk on his own.

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:11.099
X-Men destruction

00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:15.889
World War Hulk: X-Men wasn't the first clash
between the Hulk and Professor X's students,

00:06:15.889 --> 00:06:18.060
but it was definitely one of the most savage.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.250
The green giant fought one of the largest
gatherings of X-Men imaginable, and he took

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:23.409
them down.

00:06:23.409 --> 00:06:24.409
All of them.

00:06:24.409 --> 00:06:28.520
While Professor X was a member of the Illuminati
— a secret cabal of superheroes who were

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.069
the Hulk's targets during World War Hulk — the
Hulk traveled to Xavier's school to confront

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:34.069
him.

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:37.470
Rather than allow their mentor to be taken
prisoner, the X-Men fought back.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:43.139
At first it was only a small squad, but soon
all the active X-Men were facing the vengeful

00:06:43.139 --> 00:06:45.560
Hulk, including X-Factor and the Juggernaut.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:51.069
The Hulk eventually relented, but not before
breaking both of Colossus' arms and liquefying

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:55.120
Wolverine's brain by punching him in the head
over and over again.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:02.550
"Ah Hell, I don't suppose you have any interest
in talking this out."

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.150
Trolled by Hawkeye

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:09.849
Even though Hawkeye shot Bruce Banner in 2016's
Civil War 2, nobody would consider Hawkeye

00:07:09.849 --> 00:07:11.529
and The Hulk to be rivals.

00:07:11.529 --> 00:07:15.930
Still, if you look at the narrative history,
they do have kind of a weird relationship

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.139
with each other.

00:07:17.139 --> 00:07:21.099
Specifically, Hawkeye's always gone out of
his way to be a jerk to the Hulk.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:23.980
"'Cause you just made me angry?"

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:24.980
"Really?

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:28.700
Because from where I'm standing you look more
beat up than angry."

00:07:28.719 --> 00:07:33.999
After Hawkeye rage-quit the Avengers, he came
upon the Hulk battling the villain Zzzax in

00:07:33.999 --> 00:07:36.409
Incredible Hulk #166.

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:40.029
Hawkeye was so determined to show his former
teammates what he was made of that he followed

00:07:40.029 --> 00:07:43.919
and pestered the Hulk all the way into the
pages of Defenders #7.

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:48.450
He annoyed the Hulk so much that the Defenders
initially attacked Hawkeye, thinking he was

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:50.259
a villain threatening the Hulk.

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.539
Then, when Hawkeye took over the Thunderbolts,
he needed a way to boost team morale.

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:59.139
Once again, he targeted the Hulk — just
because he thought it would help their image.

00:07:59.139 --> 00:08:04.389
Four years later, in Avengers #74, Hawkeye
decided the best way to get his spot back

00:08:04.389 --> 00:08:08.479
in the Avengers was to draw blood from one
of its founding members.

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:12.550
After Banner proved that he could calm down
She-Hulk by talking to her, Hawkeye went ahead

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.669
and shot Banner anyway to force him to Hulk
out.

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:20.439
Even though he had a good reason to shoot
Banner in Civil War 2, one thing is undeniable:

00:08:20.439 --> 00:08:21.490
Hawkeye's a real jerk.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:23.360
"What do you want?"

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.140
"What do I want?

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:25.900
I wanted pickles!"

00:08:27.189 --> 00:08:28.180
Thanks for watching!

00:08:28.189 --> 00:08:30.580
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.760
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

